\section{Introduction}
Fang (ISO 639-3 \texttt{fan}) is a Bantu language spoken in Equatorial Guinea, northern Gabon and southern Cameroon, with smaller communities in the Republic of the Congo and S\~ao Tom\'e and Pr\'incipe. It is related to Bulu and Ewondo. Several orthographies exist across countries, originally developed by missionaries.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No Fang primers, textbooks or school curricula were found in the searches done.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, ``Fang language'': overview of speakers, dialects and orthography. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fang_language}
\item Omniglot, Fang: notes on the writing system. \url{https://omniglot.com/writing/fang.htm}
\item Glottolog reference record for Zabala (1887), a historical Pámue--Spanish vocabulary of Fang. \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/66800}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile of Fang in Gabon (demographic and mission-oriented information; its statements on Scripture should be checked against current Bible society listings). \url{https://www.joshuaproject.net/index.php/people_groups/11187/GB}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video, corpus or software resources were found in the searches done.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Fang is widely spoken but the search found no modern grammar, dictionary or school materials, and the orthographies differ between countries. A curriculum effort would need to settle on a standard orthography and locate current dictionaries and grammars (possibly in Spanish or French) that this search did not surface.
\end{document}
